carmen: quick heads up — hitting a weird retry/refresh loop in sampleapp when network flaps during onboarding token refresh. Seen on Android 13 + 14 emulators.

leo: what does the loop look like? multiple token requests? duplicate uploads?

carmen: flow: user signs in, client does auth refresh via synchronous call inside a suspend lambda. If network blips during RefreshRequest, Retrofit coroutine gets cancelled but OkHttp call still completes on background thread and triggers our retry interceptor. Result: two refreshes (one from the in-flight call, one retried), intermittent 409s from auth server, and failed onboarding.

zack: ah, classic cancel vs call semantics. Cancelling coroutine doesn't always cancel the underlying OkHttp call if the call was adapted badly. Which CallAdapter are you using?

carmen: using Retrofit 2 adapter for Kotlin coroutines: suspend functions via CallAdapters. Our wrapper used call.enqueue internally for non-blocking behavior, might be the culprit.

maria: can you paste the refresh snippet?

carmen: sure: 

```
suspend fun refreshToken(): TokenResponse = withContext(Dispatchers.IO) {
  try {
    api.refresh().execute().body()!! // naive sync execute inside coroutine
  } catch(e: Exception) {
    // we rely on retry interceptor to replay
    throw e
  }
}
```

zack: execute() blocks a thread, and cancellation of coroutine may not cancel that thread call unless you check isActive. Also execute() won't go through enqueue cancellation callbacks. Use Retrofit's suspend adapter or OkHttp Call.await to get proper cancellation.

leo: also check our retry interceptor: it retries on IOException without checking call.isCanceled. If coroutine cancelled but call still running, interceptor will re-attempt and double-send.

carmen: here's our retry interceptor: 
```
class SimpleRetryInterceptor : Interceptor {
  override fun intercept(chain: Interceptor.Chain): Response {
    var e: IOException? = null
    repeat(3) {
      try {
        return chain.proceed(chain.request())
      } catch (ex: IOException) {
        e = ex
      }
    }
    throw e ?: IOException("unknown")
  }
}
```

maria: that interceptor lacks cancellation checks and backoff. Also it doesn't consider HTTP 409 caused by duplicate refresh and should probably not retry token endpoints.

zack: recommended fixes: 1) use Retrofit suspend adapter (no execute()) so cancellation propagates to OkHttp; 2) make retry interceptor check Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted or chain.call().isCanceled(); 3) exclude auth/refresh endpoints from generic retry; 4) add exponential backoff with jitter.

leo: small code patch suggestion for interceptor: 
```
if (chain.call().isCanceled()) throw IOException("call canceled")
```

carmen: good call. Also saw behavior difference on Android 14 where connection pooling delays make duplicate attempts more frequent post-cold start. Might be exposed by emulator network shaping.

maria: action items:
- update sampleapp auth flow to use Retrofit suspend functions (no manual execute)
- add an example RetryInterceptor with cancellation checks + backoff
- add note in onboarding guide recommending not to retry token endpoints
- add CI e2e that simulates network flaps and ensures no duplicate refreshes

ci-bot: created ticket DEVEX-5201: "Retrofit/OkHttp cancellation mismatch causes duplicate token refresh on flaky networks" and added labels: p2, devex.

zack: I can send a minimal interceptor PR and a test harness for mocked flaky network (SocketPolicy).

carmen: thanks — I'll open a follow-up PR to sampleapp and update the quickstart. Trying to keep examples tiny so first-time users aren't overwhelmed.

leo: one more thing: mention keep-alive and connection reuse quirks in Android SDK notes. It helps explain why flakiness differs between emulators and real devices.

maria: agreed. I'll update the docs once PR lands.

carmen: closing loop — workaround already in sampleapp branch: switched to suspend API and added non-retrying path for /auth/refresh. Will update ticket with PR link.

leo: thanks all :thumbsup:
